1
00:00:00,779 --> 00:00:03,840
PART 3 chapter 1.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:08,520
He did not know where he was. Presumably he was in the Ministry of Love; but there

3
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:10,619
was no way of making certain.

4
00:00:10,619 --> 00:00:15,599
He was in a high ceiling windowless cell with walls of glittering white porcelain.

5
00:00:15,599 --> 00:00:20,279
Concealed lamps flooded it with cold light, and there was a low, steady humming

6
00:00:20,279 --> 00:00:23,580
sound which he supposed had something to do with the air supply.

7
00:00:23,580 --> 00:00:28,439
A bench, or shelf, just wide enough to sit on run around the wall, broken only by

8
00:00:28,439 --> 00:00:33,659
the door and, at the end opposite the door, a lavatory pan with no wooden seat.

9
00:00:33,659 --> 00:00:37,378
There were four telescreens, one in each wall.

10
00:00:37,378 --> 00:00:40,977
There was a dull aching in his belly. It had been there ever since they had

11
00:00:40,977 --> 00:00:44,099
bundled him into the closed van and driven him away.

12
00:00:44,099 --> 00:00:48,000
But he was also hungry, with Annoying unwholesome kind of hunger.

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,740
It might be 24 hours since he had eaten, it might be 36. He still did not know,

14
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:57,419
probably never would know, whether it had been morning or evening when they arrested him.

15
00:00:57,419 --> 00:01:01,439
Since he was arrested he had not been fed.

16
00:01:01,439 --> 00:01:04,740
He sat as still as he could on the narrow bench, with his hands crossed on

17
00:01:04,740 --> 00:01:08,040
his knee. He had already learned to sit still.

18
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:12,119
If you made unexpected movements they yelled at you from the telescreen.

19
00:01:12,119 --> 00:01:16,200
But the craving for food was growing upon him. What he longed for above all

20
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:20,099
was a piece of bread. He had an idea that there were a few breadcrumbs in the

21
00:01:20,099 --> 00:01:21,718
pocket of his overalls.

22
00:01:21,718 --> 00:01:24,957
It was even possible— —he thought this because from time to time something

23
00:01:24,957 --> 00:01:29,400
seemed to tickle his leg— —that there might be a sizable bit of crust there.

24
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:33,779
In the end of the temptation to find out overcame his fear; he slipped a hand into

25
00:01:33,779 --> 00:01:35,457
his pocket.

26
00:01:35,457 --> 00:01:38,297
'Smith!' yelled a voice from the telescreen.

27
00:01:38,297 --> 00:01:44,218
'6079 Smith W! Hands out of pockets in the cells!'

28
00:01:44,218 --> 00:01:48,718
He sat still again, his hands crossed on his knee. Before being brought here he

29
00:01:48,718 --> 00:01:51,779
had been taken to another place which must have been an ordinary prison or a

30
00:01:51,779 --> 00:01:54,360
temporary lock-up used by the patrols.

31
00:01:54,360 --> 00:01:59,159
He did not know how long he had been there; some hours, at any rate; with no clocks

32
00:01:59,159 --> 00:02:01,860
and no daylight it was hard to gauge the time.

33
00:02:01,860 --> 00:02:06,297
It was a noisy evil smelling place. They had put him into a cell similar to the

34
00:02:06,297 --> 00:02:11,580
one he was now in, but filterly dirty and at all times crowded by 10 or 15 people.

35
00:02:11,580 --> 00:02:16,319
The majority of them were common criminals, but there were a few political prisoners among them.

36
00:02:16,319 --> 00:02:21,000
He had sat silent against the wall, jostled by dirty bodies, too preoccupied

37
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,297
by fear and the pain in his belly to take much interest in his surroundings,

38
00:02:24,297 --> 00:02:27,957
but still noticing the astonishing difference in demeanor between Party

39
00:02:27,957 --> 00:02:29,759
prisoners and the others.

40
00:02:29,759 --> 00:02:34,200
The Party prisoners were always silent and terrified, but the ordinary criminals

41
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:36,058
seemed to care nothing for anybody.

42
00:02:36,058 --> 00:02:40,020
They yelled insults at the gods fought back fiercely when their belongings were

43
00:02:40,020 --> 00:02:44,159
impounded, wrote obscene words on the floor, ate smuggled food which they

44
00:02:44,159 --> 00:02:48,119
produced from mysterious hiding places in their clothes, and even shouted down

45
00:02:48,119 --> 00:02:51,360
the telescreen when it tried to restore order.

46
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:55,439
On the other hand some of them seemed to be on good terms with guards, called them

47
00:02:55,439 --> 00:02:59,759
by nicknames and tried to feed the cigarettes through the Spy hole in the door.

48
00:02:59,759 --> 00:03:03,000
The guards, too, treated the common criminals with a certain forbearance,

49
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,058
even when they had to handle them roughly.

50
00:03:06,058 --> 00:03:09,119
There was much talk about the forced labor camps to which most of the

51
00:03:09,119 --> 00:03:13,860
prisoners expected to be sent. It was 'all right' in the camps, he gathered, so long

52
00:03:13,860 --> 00:03:16,200
as you had good contact and knew the ropes.

53
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:20,099
There was bribery favoritism and racketeering of every kind, there was

54
00:03:20,099 --> 00:03:24,119
homosexuality and prostitution, there was even illicit alcohol distilled from

55
00:03:24,119 --> 00:03:28,080
potatoes. The positions of trust were given only to the common criminals,

56
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:32,520
especially the gangsters and the murderers, who formed a sort of aristocracy.

57
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:36,119
All the dirty jobs were done by the politicals.

58
00:03:36,119 --> 00:03:39,659
There was a constant common go of prisoners of every description drug

59
00:03:39,659 --> 00:03:44,218
Peddlers thieves, bandits black marketeers drunks, prostitutes.

60
00:03:44,218 --> 00:03:47,759
Some of the drunks were so violent that the other prisoners had to combine to

61
00:03:47,759 --> 00:03:52,439
suppress them. An enormous wreck of a woman, aged about 60 with great tumbling

62
00:03:52,439 --> 00:03:56,580
breasts and thick coils of white hair which had come down in her struggles, was

63
00:03:56,580 --> 00:04:00,840
carried in, kicking and shouting, by four guards who had hold of her one at each

64
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:04,378
corner. They wrenched off the boots with which she had been trying to kick them,

65
00:04:04,378 --> 00:04:08,939
and dumped her down across Winston's lap, almost breaking his thigh Bones The

66
00:04:08,939 --> 00:04:13,740
woman hoisted herself upright and followed them out with a yell of 'F— —bastards!'

67
00:04:13,740 --> 00:04:17,159
Then, noticing that she was sitting on something uneven, she slid off Winston's

68
00:04:17,159 --> 00:04:18,900
knees onto the bench.

69
00:04:18,900 --> 00:04:22,740
'Beg pardon Deary she said. 'I wouldn't have sat on you, only if the bug has put

70
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:26,040
me there. They don't know how to treat a lady, do they?'

71
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:28,340
She paused, patted her breast, and belched.

72
00:04:28,340 --> 00:04:31,797
'Pardon,' she said, 'I ain't myself quite.'

73
00:04:31,797 --> 00:04:37,797
She lent forward and vomited copiously on the floor that's better,' she said, leaning back with

74
00:04:37,797 --> 00:04:41,520
closed eyes. 'Never keep it down that's what I say.

75
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:44,878
Get it up while it's fresh on your stomach, like.'

76
00:04:44,878 --> 00:04:48,900
She revived, turned to have another look at Winston, and seemed immediately to

77
00:04:48,900 --> 00:04:53,218
take a fancy to him. She put a vast arm around his shoulder and drew him towards

78
00:04:53,218 --> 00:04:59,218
her, breathing beer and vomit into his face what's your name Deery she said.

79
00:04:59,218 --> 00:05:01,137
'Smith,' said Winston.

80
00:05:01,137 --> 00:05:06,477
'Smith?' said the woman that's funny. My name's Smith too.

81
00:05:06,477 --> 00:05:10,378
Why,' she added sentimentally, 'I might be your mother!'

82
00:05:10,378 --> 00:05:12,718
She might, thought Winston, be his mother.

83
00:05:12,718 --> 00:05:16,680
She was about the right age in physique, and it was probable that people changed

84
00:05:16,680 --> 00:05:20,457
somewhat after 20 years in a forced labor camp.

85
00:05:20,457 --> 00:05:24,957
No one else had spoken to him. To a surprising extent the ordinary criminals

86
00:05:24,957 --> 00:05:27,058
ignored the Party prisoners.

87
00:05:27,058 --> 00:05:31,378
'The pole hits they called them, with a sort of uninterested contempt.

88
00:05:31,378 --> 00:05:34,977
The Party prisoners seemed terrified of speaking to anybody, and above all of

89
00:05:34,977 --> 00:05:37,020
speaking to one another.

90
00:05:37,020 --> 00:05:40,797
Only once, when two Party members, both women, were pressed close together on the

91
00:05:40,797 --> 00:05:44,419
bench, he overheard amid to the din of voices a few hurriedly whispered words;

92
00:05:44,419 --> 00:05:50,900
and in particular a reference to something called 'room 101 which he did not understand.

93
00:05:51,297 --> 00:05:54,718
It might be two or three hours ago that they had brought him here.

94
00:05:54,718 --> 00:05:58,500
The dull pain in his belly never went away, but sometimes it grew better and

95
00:05:58,500 --> 00:06:02,700
sometimes worse, and his thoughts expanded or contracted accordingly. When

96
00:06:02,700 --> 00:06:06,840
it grew worse he thought only of the pain itself, and of his desire for food.

97
00:06:06,840 --> 00:06:10,740
When it grew better, panic took hold of him. There were moments when he foresaw

98
00:06:10,740 --> 00:06:14,159
the things that would happen to him with such actuality that his heart galloped

99
00:06:14,159 --> 00:06:15,840
and his breath stopped.

100
00:06:15,840 --> 00:06:19,619
He felt a smash of transitions on his elbows and iron shot boots on his shins;

101
00:06:19,619 --> 00:06:24,240
he saw himself groveling on the floor, screaming for mercy through broken teeth.

102
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:28,680
He hardly thought of Julia. He could not fix his mind on her. He loved her and

103
00:06:28,680 --> 00:06:32,279
would not betray her; but that was only a fact, known as he knew the rules of

104
00:06:32,279 --> 00:06:37,860
arithmetic. He felt no love for her, and he hardly even wondered what was happening to her.

105
00:06:37,860 --> 00:06:40,919
He thought often her of her brand with a flickering hope.

106
00:06:40,919 --> 00:06:43,558
O'Brien might know that he had been arrested.

107
00:06:43,558 --> 00:06:46,860
The Brotherhood, he had said, never tried to save its members.

108
00:06:46,860 --> 00:06:51,180
But there was the razor blade; they would send the razor blade if they could.

109
00:06:51,180 --> 00:06:55,200
There would be perhaps five seconds before the guard could rush into the cell.

110
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:58,977
The blade would bite into him with a sort of burning coldness, and even the

111
00:06:58,977 --> 00:07:01,680
fingers that held it would be cut to the bone.

112
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:06,477
Everything came back to his sick body, which shrank trembling from the smallest pain.

113
00:07:06,477 --> 00:07:10,439
He was not certain that he would use the razor blade even if he got the chance.

114
00:07:10,439 --> 00:07:14,340
It was more natural to exist from moment to moment, accepting another 10 minutes'

115
00:07:14,340 --> 00:07:18,860
life even with the certainty that there was torture at the end of it.

116
00:07:18,860 --> 00:07:23,819
Sometimes he tried to calculate the number of porcelain bricks in the walls of the cell.

117
00:07:23,819 --> 00:07:27,840
It should have been easy, but he always lost count at some point or another.

118
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:32,218
More often he wondered where he was, and what time of day it was. At one moment he

119
00:07:32,218 --> 00:07:35,878
felt certain that it was brought to daylight outside, and at the next equally

120
00:07:35,878 --> 00:07:38,520
certain that it was pitch darkness.

121
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:42,540
In this place, he knew instinctively, the lights would never be turned out.

122
00:07:42,540 --> 00:07:48,297
It was the place with no darkness: he saw now why O'Brien had seemed to recognize the illusion

123
00:07:48,297 --> 00:07:52,200
In the Ministry of Love there were no windows. His cell might be at the heart

124
00:07:52,200 --> 00:07:55,919
of the building or against it out a wall; it might be ten floors below ground, or

125
00:07:55,919 --> 00:07:57,240
30 above it.

126
00:07:57,240 --> 00:08:00,900
He moved himself mentally from place to place, and tried to determine by the

127
00:08:00,900 --> 00:08:06,860
feeling of his body whether he was perched high in the air or buried deep underground.

128
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:11,099
There was a sound of marching boots outside. The steel door opened with a

129
00:08:11,099 --> 00:08:15,240
clang. A young officer, a dream black uniformed figure who seemed to glitter

130
00:08:15,240 --> 00:08:20,040
all over with polished leather, and whose pale straight featured face was like a wax mask,

131
00:08:20,040 --> 00:08:22,740
stepped smartly through the doorway.

132
00:08:22,740 --> 00:08:27,058
He motioned to the guards outside to bring in the prisoner they were leading.

133
00:08:27,058 --> 00:08:32,819
The poet Ampleforth shambled into the cell. The door clanged shut again

134
00:08:32,819 --> 00:08:36,360
ample forth made one or two uncertain movements from side to side, as though

135
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:39,240
having some idea that there was another door to go out of,

136
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:42,297
and then began to wander up and down the cell.

137
00:08:42,297 --> 00:08:46,558
He had not yet noticed Winston's presence. His troubled eyes were gazing

138
00:08:46,558 --> 00:08:49,680
at the wall about a meter above the level of Winston's head.

139
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:54,419
He was shoeless; large, dirty toes were sticking out of the holes in his socks.

140
00:08:54,419 --> 00:09:00,477
He was also several days away from a shave. A scrubby beard covered his face to the cheekbones,

141
00:09:00,477 --> 00:09:06,477
giving him an air of ruffianism that went oddly with his large weak frame and nervous movements.

142
00:09:06,477 --> 00:09:11,040
Winston roused himself a little from his lethargy. He must speak to Ampleforth, and

143
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:13,080
risk the ill from the telescreen.

144
00:09:13,080 --> 00:09:20,637
It was even conceivable that Ampleforth was the bearer of the razor blade ample fourth he said.

145
00:09:20,637 --> 00:09:26,040
There was no yell from the telescreen ample forth paused, mildly startled. His

146
00:09:26,040 --> 00:09:29,520
eyes focused themselves slowly on Winston.

147
00:09:29,520 --> 00:09:33,000
'Ah, Smith!' he said. 'You too!'

148
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,159
'What are you in for?'

149
00:09:35,159 --> 00:09:40,319
'To tell you the truth— He sat down awkwardly on the bench opposite Winston

150
00:09:40,319 --> 00:09:43,319
there's only one offense is there not?' he said.

151
00:09:43,319 --> 00:09:45,180
'And have you committed it?'

152
00:09:45,180 --> 00:09:46,977
'Apparently I have.'

153
00:09:46,977 --> 00:09:50,700
He put a hand to his forehead and pressed his temples for a moment, as

154
00:09:50,700 --> 00:09:53,637
though trying to remember something.

155
00:09:53,637 --> 00:09:58,919
'These things happen,' he began vaguely I've been able to recall one instance— —a

156
00:09:58,919 --> 00:10:02,457
possible instance. It was an indiscretion, undoubtedly.

157
00:10:02,457 --> 00:10:06,297
We were producing a definitive edition of the poems of Kipling.

158
00:10:06,297 --> 00:10:09,718
I allowed the word "God" to remain at the end of a line.

159
00:10:09,718 --> 00:10:14,878
I could not help it!' he added almost indignantly, raising his face to look at Winston.

160
00:10:14,878 --> 00:10:17,340
'It was impossible to change the line.

161
00:10:17,340 --> 00:10:19,500
The rhyme was "rod".

162
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:23,878
Do you realize that there are only 12 rhymes to "rod" in the entire language?

163
00:10:23,878 --> 00:10:26,400
For days I had racked my brains.

164
00:10:26,400 --> 00:10:29,700
There was no other rhyme.'

165
00:10:29,700 --> 00:10:32,340
The expression on his face changed.

166
00:10:32,340 --> 00:10:36,779
The annoyance passed out of it and for a moment he looked almost pleased. A sort

167
00:10:36,779 --> 00:10:40,200
of intellectual warmth, the joy of the parent who has found out some useless

168
00:10:40,200 --> 00:10:44,218
fact, shone through the dirt and scrubby hair.

169
00:10:44,218 --> 00:10:48,240
'Has it ever occurred to you,' he said, 'that the whole history of English poetry has

170
00:10:48,240 --> 00:10:52,797
been determined by the fact that the English language lacks rhymes?'

171
00:10:52,797 --> 00:10:56,700
No, that particular thought had never occurred to Winston. Nor, in the

172
00:10:56,700 --> 00:11:00,500
circumstances, did it strike him as very important or interesting.

173
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:06,477
'Do you know what time of day it is?' he said ample fourth looked startled again.

174
00:11:06,477 --> 00:11:12,240
'I had hardly thought about it. They arrested me— —it could be two days ago— —perhaps three.'

175
00:11:12,240 --> 00:11:17,099
His eyes flitted round the walls, as though he half expected to find a window somewhere.

176
00:11:17,099 --> 00:11:19,977
'There is no difference between night and day in this place.

177
00:11:19,977 --> 00:11:23,040
I do not see how one can calculate the time.'

178
00:11:23,040 --> 00:11:27,119
They talked this alterally for some minutes, then, without apparent reason he

179
00:11:27,119 --> 00:11:29,878
yelled from the telescreen bad them be silent.

180
00:11:29,878 --> 00:11:34,797
Winston sat quietly, his hands crossed ample forth too large to sit in comfort

181
00:11:34,797 --> 00:11:39,119
on the narrow bench, fidgeted from side to side, clasping his lank hands first

182
00:11:39,119 --> 00:11:43,680
round one knee, then round the other. The tele screen beared him to keep still.

183
00:11:43,680 --> 00:11:48,659
Time passed 20 minutes, an hour— —it was difficult to judge.

184
00:11:48,659 --> 00:11:51,540
Once more there was a sound of boots outside.

185
00:11:51,540 --> 00:11:54,058
Winston's entrails contracted.

186
00:11:54,058 --> 00:11:58,319
Soon, very soon, perhaps in five minutes, perhaps now, the of boots would

187
00:11:58,319 --> 00:12:01,619
mean that his own turn had come.

188
00:12:01,619 --> 00:12:06,297
The door opened. The cold-faced young officer stepped into the cell.

189
00:12:06,297 --> 00:12:13,378
With a brief movement of the hand he indicated Apple forth Room 101,' he said

190
00:12:13,378 --> 00:12:17,819
ample 4th marched clumsily out between the guards, his face vaguely perturbed,

191
00:12:17,819 --> 00:12:20,840
but uncomprehending.

192
00:12:21,119 --> 00:12:26,400
What seemed like a long time passed. The pain in Winston's belly had revived. His

193
00:12:26,400 --> 00:12:30,477
mind sagged round and round on the same trick like a ball falling again and

194
00:12:30,477 --> 00:12:33,058
again into the same series of slots.

195
00:12:33,058 --> 00:12:38,700
He had only six thoughts. The pain in his belly; a piece of bread; the blood and the

196
00:12:38,700 --> 00:12:43,378
screaming; O'Brien; Julia; the razor blade.

197
00:12:43,378 --> 00:12:47,580
There was another spasm in his entrails; the heavy boots were approaching.

198
00:12:47,580 --> 00:12:51,240
As the door opened, the wave of air that it created brought in a powerful smell

199
00:12:51,240 --> 00:12:53,040
of cold sweat.

200
00:12:53,040 --> 00:12:55,378
Parsons walked into the cell.

201
00:12:55,378 --> 00:13:01,259
He was wearing khaki shorts and a sports shirt This time Winston was startled into

202
00:13:01,259 --> 00:13:03,297
self-forgetfulness.

203
00:13:03,297 --> 00:13:08,700
'You here!' he said thank you Parsons gave Winston a glance in which

204
00:13:08,700 --> 00:13:12,900
there was neither interest nor surprise, but only misery. He began walking jokely

205
00:13:12,900 --> 00:13:16,137
up and down, evidently unable to keep still.

206
00:13:16,137 --> 00:13:20,580
Each time he straightened his pudgy knees it was apparent that they were trembling.

207
00:13:20,580 --> 00:13:24,900
His eyes had a wide open staring look, as though he could not prevent himself from

208
00:13:24,900 --> 00:13:28,340
gazing at something in the middle distance.

209
00:13:28,740 --> 00:13:33,599
'What are you in for?' said Winston thought crime said Parsons, almost

210
00:13:33,599 --> 00:13:37,919
blubbering. The tone of his voice implied at once a completed mission of his guilt

211
00:13:37,919 --> 00:13:42,477
and a sort of incredulous horror that such a word could be applied to himself.

212
00:13:42,477 --> 00:13:46,200
He paused opposite Winston and began eagerly appealing to him:

213
00:13:46,200 --> 00:13:49,259
'You don't think they'll shoot me, do you all chap?

214
00:13:49,259 --> 00:13:51,957
They don't shoot you if you haven't actually done anything—

215
00:13:51,957 --> 00:13:54,419
—only thoughts, which you can't help?

216
00:13:54,419 --> 00:13:57,360
I know they give you a fair hearing.

217
00:13:57,360 --> 00:14:01,439
Oh, I trust them for that! They'll know my record, won't they?

218
00:14:01,439 --> 00:14:03,779
You know what kind of chap I was.

219
00:14:03,779 --> 00:14:05,700
Not a bad chap in my way.

220
00:14:05,700 --> 00:14:07,919
Not brainy, of course, but keen.

221
00:14:07,919 --> 00:14:10,977
I tried to do my best for the Party, didn't I?

222
00:14:10,977 --> 00:14:15,599
I'll get off with five years don't you think? Or even 10 years? A chap like me

223
00:14:15,599 --> 00:14:19,137
could make himself pretty useful in the labor camp They wouldn't shoot me for

224
00:14:19,137 --> 00:14:21,840
going off the rails just once?'

225
00:14:21,840 --> 00:14:24,477
'Are you guilty?' said Winston.

226
00:14:24,477 --> 00:14:29,580
'Of course I'm guilty!' cried Parsons with a servile glance at the telescreen. 'You

227
00:14:29,580 --> 00:14:32,819
don't think the Party would arrest an innocent man, do you?'

228
00:14:32,819 --> 00:14:38,340
His frog-like face grew Karma and even took on a slightly sanctimonious expression

229
00:14:38,340 --> 00:14:42,540
thought crime is a dreadful thing, old man,' he said sententiously. 'It's insidious.

230
00:14:42,540 --> 00:14:45,599
It can get hold of you without you even knowing it.

231
00:14:45,599 --> 00:14:48,119
Do you know how it got hold of me?

232
00:14:48,119 --> 00:14:53,340
In my sleep! Yes, that's a fact. There I was, working away, trying to do my bit—

233
00:14:53,340 --> 00:14:56,819
—never knew I had any bad stuff in my mind at all.

234
00:14:56,819 --> 00:14:59,580
And then I started talking in my sleep.

235
00:14:59,580 --> 00:15:02,759
Do you know what they heard me saying?'

236
00:15:02,759 --> 00:15:08,520
He sank his voice, like someone who is obliged for medical reasons to utter an obscenity.

237
00:15:08,520 --> 00:15:11,040
"Down with Big Brother!"

238
00:15:11,040 --> 00:15:14,819
Yes, I said that! Said it over and over again, it seems.

239
00:15:14,819 --> 00:15:18,840
Between you and me, old man, I'm glad they got me before it went any further.

240
00:15:18,840 --> 00:15:22,797
Do you know what I'm going to say to them when I go up before the tribunal?

241
00:15:22,797 --> 00:15:27,718
"Thank you," I'm going to say, "thank you for saving me before it was too late."'

242
00:15:27,718 --> 00:15:30,297
'Who denounced you?' said Winston.

243
00:15:30,297 --> 00:15:34,500
'It was my little daughter,' said Parsons with a sort of doleful pride. 'She

244
00:15:34,500 --> 00:15:36,058
listened at the keyhole.

245
00:15:36,058 --> 00:15:40,200
Heard what I was saying, and dipped off to the patrols the very next day.

246
00:15:40,200 --> 00:15:45,058
Pretty smart for an emperor of 7A I don't bear her any grudge for it. In

247
00:15:45,058 --> 00:15:49,439
fact I'm proud of her. It shows I brought her up in the right spirit, anyway.'

248
00:15:49,439 --> 00:15:54,599
He made a few more jerky movements up and down, several times casting a longing

249
00:15:54,599 --> 00:15:56,400
glance at the lavatory pan.

250
00:15:56,400 --> 00:15:58,400
Then he suddenly ripped down his shorts.

251
00:15:58,400 --> 00:16:04,020
'Excuse me, old man,' he said. 'I can't help it. It's the waiting.'

252
00:16:04,020 --> 00:16:10,259
He plumped his large posterior into the lavatory pan covered his face with his hands.

253
00:16:10,259 --> 00:16:16,740
'Smith healed the voice from the telescreen. '6079 Smith W! Uncover your

254
00:16:16,740 --> 00:16:20,400
face. No face is covered in the cells.'

255
00:16:20,400 --> 00:16:26,099
Winston uncovered his face. Parsons used the lavatory, loudly and abundantly. It

256
00:16:26,099 --> 00:16:33,137
then turned out that the plug was defective, and the cells tank abominably for hours afterwards.

257
00:16:33,297 --> 00:16:35,218
Parsons was removed.

258
00:16:35,218 --> 00:16:38,279
More prisoners came and went, mysteriously.

259
00:16:38,279 --> 00:16:43,080
One, a woman, was consigned to 'Room 101', and, Winston noticed, seemed to shrivel

260
00:16:43,080 --> 00:16:46,137
and to turn a different color when she heard the words.

261
00:16:46,137 --> 00:16:49,919
A time came when, if it had been mourning when he was brought here, it would be

262
00:16:49,919 --> 00:16:54,180
afternoon; or if it had been afternoon then it would be midnight.

263
00:16:54,180 --> 00:16:58,977
There were six prisoners in the cell, men and women. All sat very still.

264
00:16:58,977 --> 00:17:03,419
Opposite Winston they sat a man with a chinless, toothy face exactly like that

265
00:17:03,419 --> 00:17:07,860
of some large, harmless rodent. His fat, mottled cheeks were so pouched at the

266
00:17:07,860 --> 00:17:12,840
bottom that it was difficult not to believe that he had little stores of food tucked away there.

267
00:17:12,840 --> 00:17:16,860
His pale gray eyes flitted timorously from face to face, and turned quickly

268
00:17:16,860 --> 00:17:20,040
away again when he caught anyone's eye.

269
00:17:20,040 --> 00:17:23,279
The door opened, and another prisoner was brought in whose appearance sent a

270
00:17:23,279 --> 00:17:27,599
momentary chilled through Winston it was a commonplace, mean-looking man

271
00:17:27,599 --> 00:17:30,540
who might have been an engineer or technician of some kind.

272
00:17:30,540 --> 00:17:35,279
But what was startling was the emaciation of his face. It was like a skull.

273
00:17:35,279 --> 00:17:39,599
Because of its thinness the mouth and eyes looked disproportionately large, and

274
00:17:39,599 --> 00:17:45,319
the eyes seemed filled with a murderous, unappeasable hatred of somebody or something.

275
00:17:45,419 --> 00:17:49,500
The man sat down on the bench at a little distance from Winston. Winston did

276
00:17:49,500 --> 00:17:53,218
not look at him again, but the tormented skull-like face was as vivid in his mind

277
00:17:53,218 --> 00:17:56,759
as though it had been straight in front of his eyes.

278
00:17:56,759 --> 00:17:59,279
Suddenly he realized what was the matter.

279
00:17:59,279 --> 00:18:01,797
The man was dying of starvation.

280
00:18:01,797 --> 00:18:06,180
The same thoughts seemed to occur almost simultaneously to everyone in the cell.

281
00:18:06,180 --> 00:18:09,419
There was a very faint stirring all the way around the bench.

282
00:18:09,419 --> 00:18:12,659
The eyes of the chinless man kept flitting towards the skull-faced man,

283
00:18:12,659 --> 00:18:18,659
then turning guiltily away, then being dragged back by an irresistible attraction.

284
00:18:18,659 --> 00:18:21,180
Presently he began to fidget on his seat.

285
00:18:21,180 --> 00:18:25,977
At last he stood up, waddled plumsily across the cell, dug down into the pocket

286
00:18:25,977 --> 00:18:29,939
of his overalls, and, with an abashed air, held out a grimy piece of bread to the

287
00:18:29,939 --> 00:18:32,477
skull-faced man.

288
00:18:32,477 --> 00:18:37,319
There was a furious, deafening roar from the telescreen. The gymless man jumped in

289
00:18:37,319 --> 00:18:41,159
his tracks. The skull-faced man had quickly thrust his hands behind his back,

290
00:18:41,159 --> 00:18:46,159
as though demonstrating to all the worlds that he refused as the gift.

291
00:18:46,259 --> 00:18:48,619
'Bumstead!' roared the voice.

292
00:18:48,619 --> 00:18:53,637
'2713 Bumstead J! Let fall that piece of bread.'

293
00:18:53,637 --> 00:18:57,119
The gymless man dropped the piece of bread on the floor.

294
00:18:57,119 --> 00:19:02,819
'Remain standing where you are,' said the voice. 'Face the door. Make no movement.'

295
00:19:02,819 --> 00:19:07,680
The chinless man obeyed. His large pouchy cheeks were quivering uncontrollably.

296
00:19:07,680 --> 00:19:11,939
The door clanged open. As the young officer entered and stepped aside, there

297
00:19:11,939 --> 00:19:16,259
emerged from behind him a short stumpy guard with enormous arms and shoulders.

298
00:19:16,259 --> 00:19:19,919
He took his stand opposite the chinless man, and then, at a signal from the

299
00:19:19,919 --> 00:19:23,939
officer, let free a frightful blow, with all the weight of his body behind it

300
00:19:23,939 --> 00:19:26,279
fool in the chinless man's mouth.

301
00:19:26,279 --> 00:19:29,637
The force of it seemed almost to knock him clear of the floor.

302
00:19:29,637 --> 00:19:34,439
His body was flung across the cell and fetched up against the base of the lavatory seat.

303
00:19:34,439 --> 00:19:39,000
For a moment he lays those stunned, with dark blood oozing from his mouth and nose.

304
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:43,200
A very faint whimpering or squeaking, which seemed unconscious, came out of him.

305
00:19:43,200 --> 00:19:47,400
Then he rolled over and raised himself unsteadily on hands and knees.

306
00:19:47,400 --> 00:19:51,540
Amid a stream of blood and saliva, the two halves of a dental plate fell out of

307
00:19:51,540 --> 00:19:53,840
his mouth.

308
00:19:54,058 --> 00:19:57,540
The prisoners sat very still, their hands crossed on their knees.

309
00:19:57,540 --> 00:20:00,360
The chinless man climbed back into his place.

310
00:20:00,360 --> 00:20:03,360
Down one side of his face the flesh was darkening.

311
00:20:03,360 --> 00:20:07,137
His mouth had swollen into a shapeless cherry-colored mess with a black hole in

312
00:20:07,137 --> 00:20:09,137
the middle of it.

313
00:20:09,137 --> 00:20:13,860
From time to time a little blood dripped onto the breast of his overalls. His gray

314
00:20:13,860 --> 00:20:17,939
eyes still flitted from face to face, more guiltily than ever, as though he

315
00:20:17,939 --> 00:20:23,058
were trying to discover how much the others despised him for his humiliation.

316
00:20:23,819 --> 00:20:28,977
The door opened. With a small gesture the officer indicated the skull-faced man.

317
00:20:28,977 --> 00:20:31,619
'Room 101,' he said.

318
00:20:31,619 --> 00:20:35,040
There was a gasp and a flurry at Winston's side. The man had actually

319
00:20:35,040 --> 00:20:40,400
flung himself on his knees on the floor, with his hand clasped together.

320
00:20:40,400 --> 00:20:45,360
'Comrade! Officer!' he cried. 'You don't have to take me to that place! Haven't I told

321
00:20:45,360 --> 00:20:46,860
you everything already?

322
00:20:46,860 --> 00:20:51,599
What else is it you want to know? There's nothing I wouldn't confess, nothing!

323
00:20:51,599 --> 00:20:54,599
Just tell me what it is and I'll confess straight off.

324
00:20:54,599 --> 00:20:56,580
Write it down and I'll sign it— —anything!

325
00:20:56,580 --> 00:20:59,400
Not room 101!'.

326
00:20:59,400 --> 00:21:02,159
'Room 101,' said the officer.

327
00:21:02,159 --> 00:21:06,718
The man's face, already very pale, turned a color Winston would not have believed possible.

328
00:21:06,718 --> 00:21:10,340
It was definitely, unmistakably, a shade of green.

329
00:21:10,340 --> 00:21:15,180
'Do anything to me!' he yelled. 'You've been starving me for weeks. Finish it off and

330
00:21:15,180 --> 00:21:20,279
let me die. Shoot me. Hang me. Sentence me to 25 years. Is there somebody else you

331
00:21:20,279 --> 00:21:24,240
want me to give away? Just say who it is and I'll tell you anything you want.

332
00:21:24,240 --> 00:21:25,977
I don't care who it is or what you do to

333
00:21:25,977 --> 00:21:28,137
them. I've got a wife and three children.

334
00:21:28,137 --> 00:21:30,297
The biggest of them isn't six years old.

335
00:21:30,297 --> 00:21:33,419
You can take the whole lot of them and cut their throats in front of my eyes,

336
00:21:33,419 --> 00:21:35,400
and I'll stand by and watch it.

337
00:21:35,400 --> 00:21:37,680
But not room 101!'.

338
00:21:37,680 --> 00:21:40,878
'Room 101,' said the officer.

339
00:21:40,878 --> 00:21:45,058
The man looked frantically rounded the other prisoners, as though with some idea

340
00:21:45,058 --> 00:21:47,340
that he could put another victim in his own place.

341
00:21:47,340 --> 00:21:52,797
His eyes settled on the smashed face of the ginless man. He flung out a lean arm.

342
00:21:52,797 --> 00:21:56,457
'That's the one you ought to be taking, not me!' he shouted. 'You didn't hear what

343
00:21:56,457 --> 00:21:58,020
he was saying after they bashed his face.

344
00:21:58,020 --> 00:22:01,860
Give me a chance and I'll tell you every word of it. He's the one that's against

345
00:22:01,860 --> 00:22:07,137
the Party, not me.' The gods stepped forward. The man's voice rose to a shriek.

346
00:22:07,137 --> 00:22:08,878
'You didn't hear him!' he repeated.

347
00:22:08,878 --> 00:22:10,740
'Something went wrong with the telescreen.

348
00:22:10,740 --> 00:22:14,457
He's the one you want. Take him, not me!'

349
00:22:14,457 --> 00:22:18,599
The two sturdy guards had stooped to take him by the arms. But just at this

350
00:22:18,599 --> 00:22:21,840
moment he flung himself across the floor of the cell and grabbed one of the iron

351
00:22:21,840 --> 00:22:23,819
legs that supported the bench.

352
00:22:23,819 --> 00:22:26,819
He had set up a wordless howling, like an animal.

353
00:22:26,819 --> 00:22:31,319
The guards took hold of him to rent him loose, but he clung on with astonishing strength.

354
00:22:31,319 --> 00:22:35,637
For perhaps 20 seconds they were hauling at him. The prisoners sat quiet, their

355
00:22:35,637 --> 00:22:38,819
hands crossed on their knees, looking straight in front of them.

356
00:22:38,819 --> 00:22:43,500
The howling stopped; the man had no breath left for anything except hanging on.

357
00:22:43,500 --> 00:22:45,840
Then there was a different kind of cry.

358
00:22:45,840 --> 00:22:50,040
A kick from a God's boot had broken the fingers of one of his hands. They dragged

359
00:22:50,040 --> 00:22:51,718
him to his feet.

360
00:22:51,718 --> 00:22:56,340
'Room 101,' said the officer. The man was let out, walking unsteadily, with head

361
00:22:56,340 --> 00:23:01,637
sunk and nursing his crushed hand, all the fight had gone out of him.

362
00:23:01,740 --> 00:23:05,580
A long time passed. If it had been midnight when the skull-faced man was

363
00:23:05,580 --> 00:23:09,297
taken away, it was morning: if morning, it was afternoon.

364
00:23:09,297 --> 00:23:13,558
Winston was alone, and had been alone for hours. The pain of sitting on the narrow

365
00:23:13,558 --> 00:23:18,360
bench was such that often he got up and walked about, unreproved by the telescreen.

366
00:23:18,360 --> 00:23:22,259
The piece of bread still there where the chinless man had dropped it. At the

367
00:23:22,259 --> 00:23:27,360
beginning it needed a hard effort not to look at it, but presently hunger gave way to thirst.

368
00:23:27,360 --> 00:23:31,860
His mouth was sticky and evil tasting The humming sound and the unvarying

369
00:23:31,860 --> 00:23:36,297
white light induced a sort of faintness, an empty feeling inside his head.

370
00:23:36,297 --> 00:23:40,137
He would get up because the ache in his bones was no longer bearable, and then

371
00:23:40,137 --> 00:23:43,319
would sit down again almost at once because he was too dizzy to make sure of

372
00:23:43,319 --> 00:23:44,700
staying on his feet.

373
00:23:44,700 --> 00:23:48,957
Whenever his physical sensations were a little under control the terror returned.

374
00:23:48,957 --> 00:23:52,797
Sometimes with a fading hope he thought of O'Brien and the razor blade.

375
00:23:52,797 --> 00:23:57,957
It was thinkable that the Razer blade might arrive concealed in his food, if he were ever fed.

376
00:23:57,957 --> 00:24:00,119
More dimly he thought of Julia.

377
00:24:00,119 --> 00:24:03,540
Somewhere or rather she was suffering, perhaps far worse than he.

378
00:24:03,540 --> 00:24:06,297
She might be screaming with pain at this moment.

379
00:24:06,297 --> 00:24:10,740
He thought: 'If I could save Julia by doubling my own pain, would I do it?

380
00:24:10,740 --> 00:24:12,297
Yes, I would.'

381
00:24:12,297 --> 00:24:16,680
But that was merely an intellectual decision, taken because he knew that he ought to take it.

382
00:24:16,680 --> 00:24:20,700
He did not feel it. In this place you could not feel anything, except a pain

383
00:24:20,700 --> 00:24:22,619
and foreknowledge of pain.

384
00:24:22,619 --> 00:24:26,400
Besides, was it possible, when you were actually suffering it, to wish for any

385
00:24:26,400 --> 00:24:28,740
reason that your own pain should increase?

386
00:24:28,740 --> 00:24:31,619
But that question was not answerable yet.

387
00:24:31,619 --> 00:24:35,159
The boots were approaching again. The door opened.

388
00:24:35,159 --> 00:24:37,558
O'Brien came in.

389
00:24:37,558 --> 00:24:42,419
Winston started to his feet. The shock of the site had driven all caution out of him.

390
00:24:42,419 --> 00:24:47,218
For the first time in many years he forgot the presence of the telescreen.

391
00:24:47,218 --> 00:24:50,099
'They've got you too!' he cried.

392
00:24:50,099 --> 00:24:55,020
'They got me a long time ago,' said O'Brien with a mild, almost regretful irony. He

393
00:24:55,020 --> 00:24:59,279
stepped aside. From behind him there emerged abroad a chested guard with a

394
00:24:59,279 --> 00:25:01,740
long black transition in his hand.

395
00:25:01,740 --> 00:25:06,718
'You know this, Winston,' said o'bran Don't deceive yourself. You did know it—

396
00:25:06,718 --> 00:25:09,240
—you have always known it.'

397
00:25:09,240 --> 00:25:11,340
Yes, he saw now, he had always known it.

398
00:25:11,340 --> 00:25:13,619
But there was no time to think of that.

399
00:25:13,619 --> 00:25:16,797
All he had eyes for was the truncheon in the God's hand.

400
00:25:16,797 --> 00:25:20,580
It might fall anywhere: on the crown, on the tip of the ear, on the upper arm, on

401
00:25:20,580 --> 00:25:23,400
the elbow——— The elbow!

402
00:25:23,400 --> 00:25:28,500
He had slumped to his knees, almost paralyzed clasping the stricken elbow with his other hand.

403
00:25:28,500 --> 00:25:31,080
Everything had exploded into yellow light.

404
00:25:31,080 --> 00:25:35,099
Inconceivable, inconceivable that one blow could cause such pain!

405
00:25:35,099 --> 00:25:38,819
The light cleared and he could see the other two looking down at him. The guard

406
00:25:38,819 --> 00:25:40,860
was laughing at his contortions.

407
00:25:40,860 --> 00:25:42,718
One question at any rate was answered.

408
00:25:42,718 --> 00:25:47,099
Never, for any reason on earth, could you wish for an increase of pain. Of pain you

409
00:25:47,099 --> 00:25:49,977
could wish only one thing: that it should stop.

410
00:25:49,977 --> 00:25:53,099
Nothing in the world was so bad as physical pain.

411
00:25:53,099 --> 00:25:57,718
In the face of pain there are no heroes, no heroes, he thought over and over as he

412
00:25:57,718 --> 00:26:02,000
writhed on the floor, clutching uselessly at his disabled left arm
